\section{Setting up the diagnostic quizzes, literately}
\label{app:quiz}

This appendix is a literate program \autocite{LiterateProgramming}: the
prose and the code are one and the same source, woven into the text you
are reading and tangled into the files the computer runs.
It implements the pre/post-test of \cref{sec:instruments}: two quizzes
that collect two kinds of data before and after the terminal module.
The closed items measure whether the students discern the candidate
critical aspects of \cref{sec:shell,sec:filesystem,sec:composition,sec:ssh};
the open items collect the students' own accounts of the terminal, the
file system, a pipeline and a remote shell, from which the
phenomenographic analysis of \cref{sec:instruments} forms its categories
of description.
Three files are tangled from this section:
[[quiz-knowledge-start.json]] and [[quiz-knowledge-end.json]], the
descriptions of the two quizzes that the [[canvaslms]] tool pushes to
Canvas, and [[analyze_quiz.py]], which fetches the results from Canvas,
summarises the closed items and writes the open accounts to a coding
sheet.

\subsection{The two quizzes for Canvas}

The learning platform of the course is Canvas, and the [[canvaslms]]
command-line tool creates the quizzes from their JSON descriptions:
\begin{minted}{bash}
canvaslms modules create -c datintro26 -p 2 "Terminal pre-test"
canvaslms modules create -c datintro26 -p 4 "Terminal post-test"
canvaslms quizzes create -c datintro26 -f quiz-knowledge-start.json
canvaslms quizzes create -c datintro26 -f quiz-knowledge-end.json
\end{minted}
Each quiz is the only item of a module of its own, created first: the
pre-test module sits just before the terminal module and the post-test
module just after it.
The reason is that Canvas locks a module behind the completion of the
modules it names as prerequisites, and only whole modules can be
prerequisites.
With the pre-test in its own module, the terminal module can require
it, so that no student reads the material before writing the pre-test;
with the post-test in its own module, it can require the terminal
module, so that no student writes the post-test before the material.
The placement is part of the quiz description, under the key
[[modules]], which [[canvaslms]] reads and strips before it talks to
Canvas: a module name, anchored so that the pre-test module cannot match
the post-test module, and the position within it.
The modules and the quizzes are created unpublished; the teacher
releases them when the course opens.
The two quizzes share their open items and, with one exception explained
with the item (\cref{app:knowledge-items}), their knowledge items; they
differ in what opens them.
The start-of-course quiz measures what the students bring to the module;
the end-of-course quiz repeats the same items to measure the gain, and
adds one question of its own---whether the student prepared---because
preparation would confound that gain: a student who reviews the material
the night before measures preparation, not what the module left behind.
The quiz titles name the terminal explicitly, since the course runs
other diagnostic quizzes that must stay distinguishable for students and
analysis alike.
The instructions warn that some questions ask for an account in the
student's own words, so that a student who expects only multiple choice
does not skip them as odd.

<<[[quiz-knowledge-start.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Terminal quiz: what you know already",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz checks what you already know about working in a terminal, so that we can adapt the course and, together with the quiz at the end of the module, measure how well the material works. If you have not used a shell before, answer as well as you can&mdash;guessing is fine, and a low score is expected and completely okay. Five questions ask you to write in your own words; there are no wrong answers there, and they carry no points. You will see your score afterwards, but not the correct answers&mdash;the course will teach you those. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without help from others or from tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 13,
    <<start-quiz module>>
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<start result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<start-quiz consent item>>,
    <<open items>>,
    <<knowledge items>>
  ]
}
@

The end-of-course quiz carries the same open and knowledge items, so the
two administrations measure the same discernment and collect comparable
accounts; the opening item and the instructions differ, and the end quiz
adds the closed twin of the open item on a line of plain English, which
the start quiz asks only in the open form
(\cref{app:knowledge-items}).

<<[[quiz-knowledge-end.json]]>>=
{
  "quiz_type": "new",
  "settings": {
    "title": "Terminal quiz: what you know now",
    "instructions": "<p>This quiz repeats the terminal questions from the start-of-course quiz, so that we can see what you have learned during the course. Five questions ask you to write in your own words; there are no wrong answers there, and they carry no points. Afterwards you will see your score and, for each question, the correct answer. The score does not affect your grade. Please answer on your own, without preparation, help or tools.</p>",
    "points_possible": 14,
    <<end-quiz module>>
    <<scheduling settings>>
    "quiz_settings": {
      <<interaction settings>>
      <<end result view>>
    }
  },
  "items": [
    <<end-quiz preparation item>>,
    <<open items>>,
    <<item: the command line reads a language>>,
    <<knowledge items>>
  ]
}
@

The module keys name the modules created above; the quiz is the first
and only item of its module.

<<start-quiz module>>=
"modules": [{"module": "^Terminal pre-test$", "position": 1}],
<<end-quiz module>>=
"modules": [{"module": "^Terminal post-test$", "position": 1}],
@

Both quizzes are diagnostics: there is no time limit, a single attempt
suffices, and answers are never shuffled (several items show a sequence
of commands whose order is meaningful).
The items are shown one at a time, without going back, for a reason
that \cref{app:open-items} spells out: the open accounts must be
written before the student has seen the alternatives of the closed
items, and on a single page the order of the items would govern only the
order of reading.
The cost of forbidding revisits is small in a zero-stakes diagnostic; it
is the price of clean accounts.
The shared settings come in two chunks---scheduling and
interaction---and each chunk is a flat list of keys, so every brace opens
and closes in the chunk that uses it: the [[quiz_settings]] object opens
and closes in the quiz descriptions above.

<<scheduling settings>>=
"time_limit": null,
"due_at": null,
"unlock_at": null,
"lock_at": null,
<<interaction settings>>=
"shuffle_answers": false,
"shuffle_questions": false,
"has_time_limit": false,
"one_at_a_time_type": "question",
"allow_backtracking": false,
"multiple_attempts_enabled": false,
"result_view_restricted": true,
"display_items": true,
"display_item_response": true,
@

The two quizzes differ in what they show the student afterwards.
The start quiz shows the points awarded---how well the student did---but
neither per-item correctness nor the correct answers: the same questions
must measure the gain at the end of the module, so they have to stay
fresh.
The end quiz has nothing left to protect, and the students have earned
the answers: it shows, item by item, whether the answer was right and
which alternative was, so that the diagnostic also teaches---the
distractor a student chose is the misconception they can now read against
the correct account.

<<start result view>>=
  "display_item_response_correctness": false,
  "display_item_correct_answer": false,
  "display_points_awarded": true,
  "display_points_possible": true
<<end result view>>=
  "display_item_response_correctness": true,
  "display_item_correct_answer": true,
  "display_points_awarded": true,
  "display_points_possible": true
@

\subsection{The consent and preparation items}

The start-of-course quiz opens with the consent item---an arrangement
kept from the course's earlier diagnostic tests, which these quizzes
replace:
participation in the study is opt-in, so we ask before collecting
anything.
The item titles double as the keys under which Canvas reports the
answers, so the analysis program (\cref{app:quiz-analysis}) refers to
the items by these titles.

