\subsection{The knowledge items}
\label{app:knowledge-items}

The knowledge items are written for this study and published here in
full.
Each item tests one candidate critical aspect from the chapters: whether
the student discerns it is exactly what the item measures.
Following \citeauthor{NCOL}'s discussion of assessment, the stems do not
point out the relevant aspect---discerning it is what we measure
\autocite[p.~91]{NCOL}---so each stem shows a concrete command and its
behaviour and leaves the interpretation to the student.
Each distractor expresses a documented misconception or a plausible
consequence of leaving the aspect undiscerned; the prose before each
item names the aspect it tests and why the distractors are the mistakes
they are.
The correct alternative is kept about as long as the distractors, since
the longest alternative is a known cue for test-wise students.
All items weigh one point: we measure knowledge by counting correct
answers, and \cref{app:quiz-analysis} reports them item by item, since
each item stands for one aspect.

The items are numbered from position 11, so that their placement is
independent of the quiz they appear in: Canvas orders by position, gaps
are fine, and the numbering leaves room for the quiz-specific opening
item (consent or preparation) and the open accounts before them.

<<knowledge items>>=
<<item: a message after a command>>,
<<item: the same files from two sides>>,
<<item: a space in a file name>>,
<<item: which directory the command uses>>,
<<item: two names for one file>>,
<<item: where a saved file is>>,
<<item: sending output into a pipe>>,
<<item: a file on the pipe>>,
<<item: the same sort after different programs>>,
<<item: running the same commands from a file>>,
<<item: which computer answers>>,
<<item: where exit leaves you>>,
<<item: where the remote file ends up>>
@

\subsubsection{The command model}

The command line is a language, not a search box (\cref{sec:shell}): the
shell parses the line into a command and its arguments; it does not guess
what the words mean.
The item gives a line of ordinary English and asks what happens.
The correct answer reads the first word as the name of a program to run;
the search-box distractor treats the line as a query over the machine;
the remaining distractors credit the shell with understanding the words
or silently correcting them.
This item is the one the start quiz leaves out: there the same stem is
the open item \emph{what happens to this line} (\cref{app:open-items}),
because the alternatives spell out the command model---that the first
word names a program---to students who may be meeting a shell for the
first time, and the start quiz promises not to reveal what the course
will teach.
The end quiz carries both forms, so for this aspect the movement is read
from the account at the start to the item at the end, and the paired
per-item gain of \cref{app:quiz-analysis} is computed over the other
items only.
The item keeps position 11 in the end quiz; the start quiz simply has a
gap there.

<<item: the command line reads a language>>=
{
  "position": 11,
  "points_possible": 1,
  "entry": {
    "title": "The command line reads a language",
    "item_body": "<p>At the shell prompt a student types the line below, hoping to see the files in the current directory.</p><pre>$ show me my files</pre><p>What happens?</p>",
    "interaction_type_slug": "choice",
    "interaction_data": {
      "choices": [
        {"position": 1, "item_body": "<p>The shell searches the computer for anything to do with \"my files\" and lists the closest matches</p>"},
        {"position": 2, "item_body": "<p>The shell shows the files, because it understands what the words ask for</p>"},
        {"position": 3, "item_body": "<p>The shell looks for a program called show, does not find one, and reports an error</p>"},
        {"position": 4, "item_body": "<p>The shell corrects the line to the nearest real command and runs that instead</p>"}
      ]
    },
    "scoring_data": {"value": 3},
    "scoring_algorithm": "Equivalence"
  }
}
@

Textual feedback is the system's response, and an error message is
information, not failure (\cref{sec:shell}).
The item shows a command that produced a message and asks what the
message means.
The correct answer reads the message as reporting that the action did
not happen; the distractors dismiss it as an ignorable warning, blame the
terminal, or take one message to mean the shell itself has stopped.

