The shell parses the line into words at the spaces before it knows
which program runs; a name with a space in it is two words unless it is
quoted (\cref{sec:shell}).
The item shows the error that a spaced file name produces and asks why.
The correct answer reads the message as the shell having split the name;
the distractors credit [[cp]] with a one-file limit, an argument-order
rule, or a length limit---each an explanation that keeps the line whole
and blames the program, that is, that has not discerned the shell's
parsing.

